#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Query a mini4pro flight record, and fetch records off the phone.

The post-flight loop this replaces: an ad-hoc python loop per question, per record,
per night. A record is JSONL — one object per line, `t` seconds since recorder start,
`k` the kind — and beyond those two fields the shapes are heterogeneous per kind, so
this tool assumes nothing else and never silently drops a line it cannot parse.

    tools/blackbox landing06 --events                        # the flight's story
    tools/blackbox landing06 --around-event landing_commit   # everything ±2 s of it
    tools/blackbox landing06 -k tag --fields t,px,margin --every 10
    tools/blackbox landing06 -k stick_cmd --from 140 --to 148 --where "dji.throttle>0"
    tools/blackbox landing06 --code landing_commit --json    # byte-faithful, quotable
    tools/blackbox landing06 -k tag --count
    tools/blackbox landing04 landing06 --diff-events         # same path as last time?
    tools/blackbox landing06 --sql "SELECT count(*) FROM tag WHERE t < 100"
    tools/blackbox ls                                        # datasets/ inventory
    tools/blackbox ls --phone                                # what's on the phone
    tools/blackbox pull latest --as landing07                # fetch a session, verified
    tools/blackbox rm 20260728-111016 --yes                  # delete a ground stub
    tools/blackbox --selftest                                # planted records, all flags

A record is addressed by a .jsonl path (sibling parts join in), a glob, a dataset
directory, or a bare dataset name under datasets/. One session per query — .001/.002
are parts of one recorder run and concatenate with t continuous; a different
timestamp prefix is a different recorder run, and two of those only ever meet in
--diff-events / --diff-counts.

Division of labour, on purpose: **blackbox queries, flightlog diagnoses.** There is
no --summary here beyond a pointer — `tools/flightlog <record> --summary` is the
session overview and health report, and duplicating it would fork it.

Time flags: --from/--to are absolute seconds; --around T and --around-event CODE take
±WINDOW seconds around the centre (default ±2.0 — note flightlog's --window is a full
width; here it is the half-width, because "±2 s around landing_commit" is the sentence
being typed). Reduction order is fixed: filters, then --every, then --tail, then
--limit — so `--tail 10 --limit 5` is the first five of the last ten.

Output: aligned table by default, with a footer counting matches, lines, files and
unparseable lines; --json prints the matching lines byte-for-byte (that is what makes
blackbox quotable in measurement docs); --csv for spreadsheets. --sql runs DuckDB over
view `rec` (file, lineno, t, k, raw JSON) plus one view per kind whose JSON fields are
real columns: `SELECT t, code, msg FROM event WHERE code='landing_commit'`, `sum(CASE
WHEN px>=60 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) FROM tag`. Nested objects are STRUCT columns under
their own name — `f.vx` on mav_out reads the nested field directly; a field whose type
varies across rows (the recorder writes non-finite floats as strings, e.g. tag.e2)
stays JSON rather than being coerced. DuckDB lives in the DiMOS venv, and blackbox
re-execs itself there for --sql only; every other mode is stdlib python3.

Phone: records live on the phone under .../files/flightlogs, reachable ONLY through
the tools/phoneadb tunnel (laptop↔phone adb is blackholed by the AP — wifi-fix.md).
`pull` verifies every file's size against the phone afterwards, because a tunnel drop
mid-pull truncates silently; `rm` lists what it will delete, requires --yes, and
refuses `rm latest` by name.

Exit codes: 0 matched something / succeeded, 1 matched nothing, 2 refusal or error.

The Python API with the same powers: tools/blackbox_lib.py (this file is a thin
shell over it).
"""

